@rem Exercises for lesson 07_exam_tips (C++ track). Format: docs/CONTENT_FORMAT.md

@exercise cpp-exam-choice-compile
@type choice
@lesson 07_exam_tips
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Four of your five functions are correct, but the file has a compile error. What is the result?
@choices
- 80 percent
- 4 out of 5 functions are marked
* 0 for the whole exam
- Only the broken function gets 0
@explain
A compile error, an infinite loop or a crash gives 0 for the whole exam.

@exercise cpp-exam-choice-wrong
@type choice
@lesson 07_exam_tips
@difficulty 1
@prompt
One function compiles and finishes but returns a wrong result. What happens?
@choices
* Only that function fails
- The whole exam gets 0
- The file is not marked
- All functions after it fail
@explain
A wrong result fails only that function.

@exercise cpp-exam-choice-unfinished
@type choice
@lesson 07_exam_tips
@difficulty 1
@prompt
You cannot finish a function in time. What is the safest thing to do?
@choices
- Leave the half-written loop in the file
- Delete the function from the file
* Make it compile and terminate with a placeholder return value
- Comment out the tests at the bottom
@explain
A function that compiles and ends can at worst fail by itself. Half a loop can break the build or never end, and then the whole exam is 0.

@exercise cpp-exam-choice-not-fatal
@type choice
@lesson 07_exam_tips
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Which of these costs you the whole exam?
@choices
- One function returns a wrong result
- Two functions return wrong results
* A program that crashes because a pointer is dereferenced while it is nullptr
- A function that is correct but written in a different style
@explain
A wrong result fails only that function. A compile error, an infinite loop or a crash gives 0 for the whole exam.

@exercise cpp-exam-theory-semicolon
@type theory
@lesson 07_exam_tips
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Which character must follow the closing brace of a class definition?
@answer
; | a semicolon | semicolon
@explain
A missing semicolon after a class is one of the classic compile errors, and a compile error gives 0 for the whole exam.

@exercise cpp-exam-theory-nullptr
@type theory
@lesson 07_exam_tips
@difficulty 1
@prompt
What should a pointer hold when it has no target yet, so that you can test it safely before using it?
@answer
nullptr | a null pointer | null pointer
@explain
Set unused pointers to nullptr and test them before using ->. Dereferencing an invalid pointer is a crash.

@exercise cpp-exam-predict-nullptr
@type predict
@lesson 07_exam_tips
@difficulty 1
@prompt
What does this code print?
@code
int* p = nullptr;
if (p == nullptr) {
    cout << "empty";
} else {
    cout << *p;
}
cout << endl;
@answer
empty
@explain
Testing the pointer first avoids the crash: nothing is dereferenced when p is nullptr.

@exercise cpp-exam-fill-placeholder
@type fill
@lesson 07_exam_tips
@difficulty 1
@prompt
You have no time to finish isEmpty. Make it compile and end by returning a placeholder value.
@code
bool isEmpty() {
    // not finished yet: keep the file compiling and ending
    ___ false;
}
@answer
return
@explain
A function that compiles and ends can at worst fail by itself. Half a loop can break the build or never end, and that costs the whole exam.

@exercise cpp-exam-bug-list-loop
@type bug
@lesson 07_exam_tips
@difficulty 2
@prompt
This code should add up the list, but the program never ends. Click the line of the loop that never finishes (either of the two lines involved is accepted).
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
};

int main() {
    Node* second = new Node{2, nullptr};
    Node* head = new Node{1, second};
    int sum = 0;
    Node* cur = head;
    while (cur != nullptr) {
        sum += cur->data;
    }
    cout << sum << endl;
    delete second;
    delete head;
    return 0;
}
@bugline 14 15
@explain
cur is never moved to the next node, so cur != nullptr stays true forever. Add cur = cur->next; inside the loop. An infinite loop gives 0 for the whole exam.

@exercise cpp-exam-bug-array-bounds
@type bug
@lesson 07_exam_tips
@difficulty 2
@prompt
This code should print the three numbers but reads past the end of the array, which can crash the program. Click the buggy line, then type the corrected line.
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int numbers[3] = {10, 20, 30};
    for (int i = 0; i <= 3; i++) {
        cout << numbers[i] << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;
    return 0;
}
@bugline 6
@answer
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
@explain
The valid indexes are 0, 1 and 2. i <= 3 also reads numbers[3], which is out of bounds.
